    "result_text": "That's just JaCoCo coverage excludes \u2014 not test excludes. The implementation is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country E.164 national-number length validation on the owner-create path.\n\n**What the spec required:** validate that an E.164 telephone's *national* digit count matches its country code (`+61` \u2192 9 national digits, `+1` \u2192 10), rejecting mismatches with HTTP 400.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **New `E164Country` enum** (`rest/validation/E164Country.java`) \u2014 owns the per-country knowledge as a single-responsibility unit: each entry pairs a calling code (`61`, `1`) with its required national-number length (9, 10), plus a `forDigits(...)` lookup that matches the longest calling-code prefix.\n\n2. **`TelephoneNormalizer`** \u2014 refactored to cleanly separate the calling code from the national number instead of lumping everything into one `national` string. After the existing 8\u201315 total-digit sanity check, it now enforces the exact national length for the recognized country (the given `+` country code, or the default `+61` when none is supplied). Unrecognized country codes keep the prior lenient behavior.\n\nThe existing 400 wiring already covered this: `TelephoneNormalizer.normalize(...)` runs in `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`, and `InvalidTelephoneException` is mapped to 400 by `ExceptionControllerAdvice`, so no changes were needed there.\n\n**Verification:** the new acceptance test (`+61 123` \u2192 400) passes, and the full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`). The valid-owner payload (`04XXXXXXXX` \u2192 drops the `0` \u2192 9 national digits under `+61`) still normalizes and passes all telephone rules.",
